    pr_ref:  # 1778
    description: "`setup.py`'s `MANAGED_BLOCK_CONTENT` — the governance text `htrac setup`\ninjects into an ADOPTING repo's AGENTS.md — documents two statuses that the\ntracker does not accept:\n\n    **Resolution Rationale:** When changing a tracker item to a resolved state\n    (`done`, `holding`, `wontfix`), always record WHY ...\n\nand a status table row `| `wontfix` | No | Decided against |`.\n\n  - `holding` is DEPRECATED. This repo's own AGENTS.md says so explicitly\n    (\"Do NOT use `holding` (deprecated) — it is ambiguous and will be rejected\n    by the tracker\"), and `tag_catalog.py` cites it as the precedent for how\n    deprecated values are retained-but-refused.\n  - `wontfix` was never a status. `models.py` defines\n    `resolved_statuses = [\"done\", \"wont_do\", \"deleted\"]`. Outside setup.py's\n    prose, the string `wontfix` appears nowhere in the tracker source.\n\nSo an adopting repo's agents are told to write a value that is rejected, and\nthe failure surfaces as a mutation that does not take.\n\nDISCOVERED alongside WI-dolaf (the `--json` flag position), in the same string\nconstant, and deliberately NOT folded into that fix: different mechanism —\na stale enum, not an argument-order hazard — and folding it in would have\nwidened a documentation PR into a vocabulary change without its own review.\n\nFIX: correct the block to `done` / `wont_do`, drop the `wontfix` table row (or\nmark it deprecated the way `holding` is handled), and check whether the same\nblock states anything else about the status vocabulary that has since moved.\nGovernance-adjacent: it ships outward to adopters, so it warrants a look at\nwhether any adopting repo has already copied it."  # 1778
    fields: {}  # 1778
